rows.elements_checklist:
1.5(1) (1) the service requires the Staff to drive the Person and then Staff must be at least 18 years of age to provide transportation;
1.5(2) (2) the Person is a client of DHHS Division of Juvenile Justice and Youth Services ("DJJYS") or Division of Child and Family Services ("DCFS") in which case the Staff must be at least 18 years of age; or
1.5(3) (3) otherwise specified in the individual service code descriptions.

1.6(3)(A) (A) understanding of the Person's needs;
1.6(3)(C) (C) confidentiality requirements regarding the Person and the Person's information;
1.6(3)(D) (D) emergency procedures;
1.6(3)(E) (E) training specific to the individual services the volunteer will be providing; and

1.8(4)(A) (A) when to call 911;
1.8(4)(B) (B) when to call a medical professional;
1.8(4)(C) (C) when to call a mental health professional;
1.8(4)(D) (D) incident reporting;
1.8(4)(E) (E) basic orientation about seizure disorders, including what to do if a Person not known to have seizures has a seizure;
1.8(4)(F) (F) notification procedures for when the whereabouts of a Person are unknown;
1.8(4)(G) (G) common rescue maneuvers for choking such as the Heimlich maneuver;
1.8(4)(H) (H) prevention of choking;
1.8(4)(I) (I) the use of positive behavior supports as a first response in behavioral crisis prevention and intervention in accordance with Utah Administrative Code R539-4;
1.8(4)(J) (J) legal rights of Persons relevant to the Staff's responsibilities, and how the Americans with Disabilities Act relates to the rights of Persons receiving services;
1.8(4)(K) (K) abuse, neglect, and exploitation prevention and reporting to protective services and the police;
1.8(4)(L) (L) confidentiality regarding all Person information is shared only with individuals who need to know the information to provide support or professional treatment, coordinate DHHS services, ensure safety, or conduct DHHS business. Person information must be maintained and shared in compliance with Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act ("HIPAA") regulations;
1.8(4)(M) (M) orientation to ID.RC and ABI;
1.8(4)(N) (N) prevention of communicable diseases;
1.8(4)(O) (O) Person-specific training on each Person the Staff will provide services to, including:
1.8(4)(O)(i) (i) the Person's disability, interests, goals, and support needs; (ii) relevant medical, health, and safety information including indications of when the Person may be getting sick, experiencing a decline in mental health, other mental health considerations, choking risks, dietary needs and supports, seizures and details regarding seizure-related support, supervision needs and details regarding supervision needs; (iii) applicable portions of the Person's PCSP, BSP, Individualized Education Plan, Employment Plan, suicide prevention safety plan, and emergency safety protocols; (iv) the Staff's responsibilities regarding providing support to the Person and when to seek additional support from supervisors;
1.8(4)(O)(v) (v) Do Not Resuscitate ("DNR") protocols, when applicable; (vi) Physician Orders for Life Sustaining Treatment ("POLST") protocols, when applicable; (vii) medical orders and treatment protocols when Persons are receiving palliative care; and (viii) hospice company protocols on end of life or comfort care when Persons are receiving hospice services.
1.8(4)(P) (P) the Contractor's policies, procedures, and plans relevant to the services the Staff will be providing;
1.8(4)(Q) (Q) introduction to DSPD philosophy, mission, and beliefs;
1.8(4)(R) (R) applicable portions of the DHHS Medicaid 101 training;
1.8(4)(S) (S) reporting suspected fraud, waste, abuse, and mismanagement to the Utah Office of Inspector General ("OIG") at https://oig.utah.gov/report-fraud/;
1.8(4)(T) (T) HCBS Settings Rule;
1.8(4)(U) (U) crisis de-escalation strategies;
1.8(4)(V) (V) trauma-informed care; and
1.8(4)(W) (W) suicide prevention.

1.8(5)(A) (A) first aid (Staff must receive and maintain current first aid certification);
1.8(5)(B) (B) cardiopulmonary resuscitation (Staff must receive and maintain current cardiopulmonary resuscitation certification); and
1.8(5)(C) (C) Person-Centered thinking and practices.

1.8(6)(A) (A) Supports Options and Actions for Respect ("SOAR");
1.8(6)(B) (B) System for Managing Non-Aggressive and Aggressive People ("MANDT");
1.8(6)(C) (C) Professional Assault Response Training ("PART");
1.8(6)(D) (D) Crisis Prevention Institute ("CPI") or Safety Care; or
1.8(6)(E) (E) another intervention-training program with prior written approval from DSPD.

1.8(8)(A) (A) effects of brain injuries on behavior;
1.8(8)(B) (B) transitioning from hospitals to community support programs, including available resources;
1.8(8)(C) (C) functional impact of brain injury;
1.8(8)(D) (D) health and medication effects specific to Persons with ABI;
1.8(8)(E) (E) role of the direct-care and direct-care supervisory Staff relating to the treatment and rehabilitation process; and
1.8(8)(F) (F) awareness of the family's perspective on the brain injury.

1.9(1) (1) the Staff's name, addresses, and telephone number;
1.9(3) (3) documentation of successfully completed trainings;
1.9(5) (5) copies of regular performance evaluations;
1.9(6) (6) Medicaid Disclosure Forms found on the DSPD webpage; and

1.10(1) (1) the Person's name, address, phone number, birth date, identification number, and, if applicable, Medicaid number;
1.10(2) (2) the Person's Support Coordinator's name, email, and phone number;
1.10(3) (3) a current photograph of the Person taken within the last five years;
1.10(4) (4) the name, address, and phone number of the Person's representative or guardian, if any, and name, address, and phone number for the Person's emergency contacts, including instructions on how to contact them;
1.10(5) (5) the name and phone number of the Person's primary health care professional, medical specialist, health care professional prescribing medication, if any, and medical insurance information;
1.10(6) (6) documentation of approved charges or expenses placed against the Person's funds for reimbursement to the Contractor for property damages for which the Person is held responsible;
1.10(8) (8) Person Centered Planning documentation;
1.10(9) (9) the Person's admission and discharge dates;
1.10(10) (10) legal documents including: Human Rights Committee and Behavior Peer-Review Committee documentation; guardianship/legal representation appointments; and any other relevant legal documents;
1.10(12) (12) the Person's relevant medical information, as per the Health Supports Policies and Procedures section of this contract, and as identified in the individual service code descriptions;
1.10(13) (13) all of the Person's individualized plans; and

1.10(7)(A) (A) the name of the Person receiving the service;
1.10(7)(B) (B) the date the service was delivered;
1.10(7)(C) (C) the start time and end time the service was delivered;
1.10(7)(D) (D) the name of the DHHS service code that was delivered;
1.10(7)(E) (E) the name of the Staff providing the service; and
1.10(7)(F) (F) a written summary note of substantive service activities consistent with the individual service description.

1.18(1) (1) clearly defined Staff and supervisory responsibilities during all hours of operation;
1.18(3) (3) provisions for the receipt and resolution of Staff grievances and Persons grievances that comply with this contract;
1.18(4) (4) emergency procedures for handling the injury, illness, indications of when the Person may be getting sick or experiencing a decline in mental health, or death of a Person and instructions about when and how to notify necessary individuals, including when and how to notify the DSPD Waiver managers according to fatality, critical incident reports, and critical incident investigations sections of this contract;
1.18(6) (6) with the exception of Personal Budget Assistance and Homemaker services, how the Contractor will comply with Utah Administrative Code R539-4 with regard to BSPs and behavior intervention procedures; and

1.20(b)(1) (1) an identification of a specific and individualized assessed need;
1.20(b)(2) (2) the positive intervention and support used prior to any modifications to the PCSP;
1.20(b)(3) (3) less intrusive methods of meeting the need that have been tried but did not work
1.20(b)(4) (4) a clear description of the condition that is directly proportionate to the specific assessed need;
1.20(b)(5) (5) a regular collection and review of data to measure the ongoing effectiveness of the modification;
1.20(b)(7) (7) informed consent of the Person; and
1.20(b)(8) (8) an assurance that interventions and supports will cause no harm to the Person.
